<DocsLayout title="Advanced Haystack guide | dotfetch" description="Approval through ConfirmationHook, per-step selection, run context and error modes for the dotfetch Haystack integration.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Advanced framework guide</p>
  <h1>Advanced Haystack</h1>
  <p class="lead">Approval with <code>ConfirmationHook</code>, how per-step selection works, and run context.</p>
  <p><a class="pill" href="/dotfetch/docs/frameworks/haystack/">← Basic Haystack usage</a></p>

  <h2>Approval with ConfirmationHook</h2>
  <p>A confirmation-gated tool (<code>requires_confirmation=True</code> or a <code>SecurityPolicy(require_confirmation=[...])</code> pattern) is confirmed through Haystack's human-in-the-loop machinery. <code>adapter.confirmation_hook(ui)</code> is a <code>before_tool</code> hook that asks the <code>ConfirmationUI</code> about gated dotfetch tools only.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`from haystack.hooks.human_in_the_loop import RichConsoleUI

agent = HaystackAdapter(fetcher).agent(chat_generator=generator, confirmation_ui=RichConsoleUI())

# Or, with tools you selected yourself:
agent = Agent(
    chat_generator=generator,
    tools=await adapter.select("refund an order"),
    hooks={"before_tool": [adapter.confirmation_hook(RichConsoleUI())]},
)`} />
  <p>A confirmed call runs once through <code>fetcher.execute</code> with only the confirmation gate cleared; a modified one runs once with the new arguments; a rejected one never runs and the model is told. Each approval is tied to that exact call on the run's <code>State</code>. Without a confirmation hook, or when a tool is called directly, a gated call reports that it needs approval.</p>

  <h2>Per-step selection</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> <code>agent()</code> gives the <code>Agent</code> a dynamic <code>Toolset</code> plus <code>before_run</code> and <code>after_tool</code> hooks. Haystack re-flattens toolsets at the start of every step, so each model call after a tool step sees the new selection.</li>
    <li><strong>Query:</strong> the latest user message, plus the latest successful tool result (<code>query_from_results=True</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Sticky:</strong> tools offered earlier in the run stay, up to <code>max_offered</code> (default <code>3 * limit</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Failure:</strong> if retrieval fails the run keeps its previous tools plus the pins, and a warning is logged.</li>
    <li>Per-run state travels in a context variable, so concurrent runs of one Agent stay apart. Your own <code>hooks</code> still run.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Run context and tracing</h2>
  <p>Haystack injects the Agent's live <code>State</code> into each dotfetch tool (the model's schema never shows it). <code>current_agent_state()</code> returns it; <code>current_tool_invocation()</code> carries it too, with <code>deps</code> set to <code>state.data</code>. Tools have a native <code>async_function</code>, so <code>Agent.run_async</code> does not block. The active OpenTelemetry span becomes the dotfetch <code>ToolCall</code>'s trace.</p>
</DocsLayout>
